There are three programs written in 'C' on this diskette. They are
public domain programs and may be modified and distributed as you see
fit. The three programs are TABIFY, PRINT, and CALC.

TABIFY processes an input file combining a group of spaces into tabs
where ever posible. The form of the command is:

	TABIFY INPUTFILE OUTPUTFILE
  ex:	TABIFY BIOS.LST BIOS.TAB

PRNT processes an input file expanding tabs and sends it to an output
file which may be the printer if PRN: is specified. PRNT additionally
allows addition of a left margin and top margin.The values of these are
specified after PRNT starts execution. If no margins are desired enter
0 when prompted. The form of the command is:

	PRNT INPUTFILE OUTPUTFILE
  ex:	PRNT BIOS.LST PRN:	   Prints the LST file on the printer
	PRNT BIOS.ASM BIOS.PRN    processes the file for later dumping to ptr
	PRNT BIOS.ASM CON:        copies the file to the console for viewing
				   before printing

CALC is a simple calculator which uses reverse Polish notation. The
functions provided are +, -, *, and /. The following is a sample of use:

	CALC
	2 2 + =
		4.0000000
	2 * =
		8.0000000

FUNCTIONS:
	
	-	minus
	+	addition
	*	multiplication
	/	division
	^	exponentiation
	c	clear stack
	=	print top stack element and leave on stack

The following functions have been added to the calculator:
	
	sin	sin of an angle expresed in radians
	cos	cos of an angle expresed in radians
	tan	tangent of an angle expresed in radians
	asin	the inverse sin
	acos	the inverse cosine
	atan	the inverse tangent
	sqrt	square root of the top of the stack
	exp	e raised to the value on the top of stack e^st
	ln	the natural log of the stack top
	loq	log10 of the stack top
	pow	10^st (10 raised to the value of the stack top (inv log)
	swap	swap the top of stack and stack top -1
	inv	replace stack top with 1/stack top
	pop	pop the top of stack
	clr	clear the whole stack
	quit	exit to dos

Any comments on these programs should be written. These programs are not
supported by LDP, and are included only as examples of useful programs 
written in 'C'.
